rname><given-names>M. J.</given-names></name></person-group> (<year>1998</year>). <article-title>Measuring presence in virtual environments: a presence questionnaire</article-title>. <source>Presence-Teleop. Virt.</source> <volume>7</volume>, <fpage>225</fpage>&#x02013;<lpage>240</lpage></mixed-citation></ref></ref-list><glossary><title>Key Concept</title><def-list><def-item><term id="G1">Presence</term><def><p>Presence is understood as referring to the subjective feeling of being in a virtual environment while transiently unaware of one's real location and surroundings and of the technology that delivers the stream of virtual input to the senses</p></def></def-item></def-list></glossary><bio><p><inline-graphic xlink:href="fnins-03-052-i001.gif"/><bold>Lutz J&#x000e4;ncke</bold> is Professor of Neuropsychology at the University Zurich. During the past 25 years he has conducted research on many different topics in cognitive neuroscience, ranging from cognitive neuroanatomy, brain plasticity, sensorimotor control and the neuroscientific basis of music perception and production. Beside these main research branches he has also developed a strong interest in studying the neural basis of presence experience. In addition to his research, he is an active clinical neuropsychologist.</p></bio></back></article>